{"name":"Deep River","hex":"#0079b3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Deep River","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A river in the Great Southern region of Western Australia.","Some rivers in the United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Deep_River"}],"entries":[{"title":"Deep River (Western Australia)","blocks":[{"text":"The Deep River is a river in the Great Southern region of Western Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Fernhook_falls.jpg/330px-Fernhook_falls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_River_(Western_Australia)"},{"title":"Deep River (Indiana)","blocks":[{"text":"Deep River is a river that flows 29.5 miles (47.5 km) northeast to the Little Calumet River in Lake County in northern Indiana in the United States. It is part of a fluvial system that drains about 10% of northern Indiana to Lake Michigan at Port of Indiana-Burns Harbor."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Lake_George_P5200053.JPG/330px-Lake_George_P5200053.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_River_(Indiana)"},{"title":"Deep River (Hikaru Utada album)","blocks":[{"text":"Deep River is the fourth studio album by Japanese-American singer Hikaru Utada. It was released via Toshiba EMI on June 19, 2002. The title of the album, as well as its title track, was inspired by the novel Deep River by Shusaku Endo. Utada wrote and co-produced the majority of the record, and unlike their previous album Distance (2001), they worked primarily with Japanese collaborator Akira Miyake and Teruzane Utada. Musically, Deep River is widely noted by fans and critics as the transition state from Utada's earlier style, R&B, to ethereal pop."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/70/UtadaHikaruDEEPRIVER.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_River_(Hikaru_Utada_album)"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Deep_river.jpg/500px-Deep_river.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Deep_river.jpg/960px-Deep_river.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Deep_river.jpg/500px-Deep_river.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeep_river.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Wood%27s_Grist_Mill_at_Deep_River_County_Park.jpg/500px-Wood%27s_Grist_Mill_at_Deep_River_County_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wiki